  "afd": {
    "available": true,
    "issued_at": "2026-10-06T05:15:00+00:00",
    "product_id": "4acf40fd-4742-4360-af72-6c65179c22cc",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 060515\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n1015 PM MST Mon Oct 5 2026\n\n.UPDATE...\nUpdated Aviation\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Above normal temperatures with minimal day to day changes will \nexist through the majority of the week as afternoon highs reach 97 \nto 103 degrees across the lower deserts.\n\n- An Extreme Heat Watch is in effect for portions of southeast \nCalifornia and southwest Arizona on Wednesday and Thursday as \ntemperatures warm to near record levels and lead to major HeatRisk.\n\n- Dry weather with zero rain chances will persist through Saturday \nwith shower and storm chances returning to Arizona as early as \nSunday.\n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/... \nIt has been another breezy day across southern AZ, but winds have \nnot quite as strong as yesterday due to a weakening pressure \ngradient. Surface pressure to the east is lowering which is allowing \nthe east-west gradient to relax. This weakening will continue \nthrough tomorrow morning. So, there will still be some breeziness in \nthe morning tomorrow before the gradient fully relaxes and winds \nreturn to a more typical light diurnal pattern.\n\nHigh pressure remains in place over the Southwest, with midday \nobjective analysis showing H5 heights around 591dam, which is just \nabove the 90th percentile based on sounding climatology. This high \npressure, and mostly clear skies, will continue to support above \nnormal temperatures across the region. Latest aircraft soundings out \nof Phoenix show little change in the low-level thermal profile over \nthe last 24 hours. So, high temperatures this afternoon will be \nnearly identical to yesterday, with lower deserts topping out around \n97-103 degrees.\n\nHeading through tomorrow and Wednesday a weak shortwave currently \nover northern NV is expected to turn southeast and drop through \nnortheast AZ on Wednesday while a strong anticyclone develops near \nthe coast of SoCal. While the shortwave may lead to isolated showers \nand storms up along the Mogollon Rim Wednesday, the lower deserts \nare expected to remain dry. The shortwave will also help keep south-\ncentral AZ from seeing a significant warm-up, unlike areas to the \nwest. Temperatures tomorrow will still be similar to today, if not a \ndegree warmer, and then temperatures warm 2-4 degrees into \nWednesday, with nearly all lower desert locations reaching 100 \ndegrees in the afternoon. Temperatures will be hottest across \nsoutheast CA and along the Lower Colorado River Valley, including \nYuma, where highs on Wednesday will reach the 104-107 degree range, \nwhich is upwards of 11-14 degrees above normal for early October and \nwill be near daily records. An Extreme Heat Watch remains in effect \nstarting Wednesday for the Imperial Valley, CA and Yuma, AZ.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /THURSDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/... \nA strong anticyclone is expected to persist near the SoCal coast \nthrough at least Thursday, which will keep temperatures well above \nnormal and near daily records across SoCal. Temperatures are also \nexpected to warm another 1-2 degrees across south-central AZ on \nThursday, following the passage of a weak shortwave trough. Even \nsouth-central AZ will see high temperatures up to 10 degrees above \nnormal (99-102 degrees) on Thursday. An Extreme Heat Watch remains \nin effect through Thursday for Imperial Valley, CA and Yuma, AZ, \nwhere probabilities of Major HeatRisk are highest (50-70%). This \nwatch is likely to be upgraded to a warning tonight or tomorrow \nmorning and Friday is being considered for the heat alert as well. \nLatest NBM forecast shows 30-50% Major HeatRisk probs on Friday for \nthe same areas under the current watch. \n\nThe coastal anticyclone is expected to breakdown heading into Friday \nas the synoptic pattern shifts, with a Pacific low moving toward the \nCA coast, but low level temperatures are expected to remain very \nwarm. Latest global ensembles show 850mb temps cooling by only a \ndegree or two centigrade into Friday, which should only result in 1-\n4 degrees of surface cooling from Thursday. Latest high temperature \nforecast for Friday has 101-106 degrees across southeast CA and \nsouthwest AZ to 97-101 degrees across south-central AZ.\n\nAs alluded to, the pattern will shift heading into the end of this \nweek and this weekend with a trend toward troffing and negative \nheight anomalies across the Western CONUS. While there is good \nconfidence in the trough pattern developing, there is still a lot of \nmodel uncertainty/spread regarding how the trough pattern will \nevolve heading into the end of the weekend and start of next week.\nIt does look like the Pacific low that will near the CA coast on \nFriday will dip south this weekend and interact or faze with TC \nRachel while at the same time a trough will move out of the Gulf of \nAlaska and strengthen as it moves south down the northern Pacific \ncoast. There are certainly a lot of eyes on what will happen with TC \nRachel and whether or not it will help bring rain to the Desert \nSouthwest, just like with TCs Odalys and Polo early last week. \n\nLatest guidance has trended toward an eventual turn to the northeast \nby Rachel by the end of the weekend. While this trend does lend \nitself in favor of a push of moisture toward the Southwest CONUS, \nthere is still a lot of uncertainty in the forecast this far out, \nespecially with timing and evolution of the trough moving down the \nnorthern Pacific coast and how that may affect the remnants of \nRachel. Current thinking is at least mid and high level moisture \nwill eventually shear off or Rachel up toward the Southwest, which \nmay bring showery activity as early as Sunday and then the more \npotent coastal trough/low will help drive additional shower and \nstorm chances across AZ, while also sending a cold front and cooler \ntemperatures through the region early next week. Again uncertainty \nis still high and it is ill-advised to buy into single deterministic \nlong-range model runs that may show a strong tropical circulation \npushing into the U.S.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 0515Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\nNo weather concerns will exist through Tuesday night under \noccasional midlevel cloud decks. Confidence is excellent that \neasterly winds will persist well into Tuesday afternoon before \neventually switching to the traditional westerly direction late \nafternoon/early evening. \n\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\nNo weather issues will exist through Tuesday night under clear \nskies. Winds will generally maintain some version of a northerly \ncomponent, however extended periods of light and variable winds will \nbe common.  \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nDry conditions with well above normal temperatures will continue \nthrough the week, with afternoon high temperatures reaching up to \n100-107 degrees across the lower deserts (hottest on Thursday). \nBreezy easterly winds will continue across southern AZ through this \nafternoon and Tuesday morning, with gusts up to 20-25 mph. Winds \nwill return to their more typical light diurnal tendencies  Tuesday \nafternoon through Thursday, with only minor afternoon upslope \ngustiness. A gusty easterly gradient wind is expected to develop \nagain by Friday and last through Saturday. MinRHs will mostly range \nfrom 15-25% across the western districts to 20-35% across eastern \ndistricts through the week with fair to good overnight recovery (35- \n60%) area-wide. Humidities will be lowest on Thursday when \ntemperatures are likely at their hottest, but winds will still be \nlight at this time, so there are no elevated fire weather concerns. \nNo wetting rain expected through at least Saturday. Rain chances \nthen go up by the end of the weekend/start of next week with \nincreased moisture and a potential weather system moving through.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...Extreme Heat Watch from Wednesday morning through Thursday \n     evening for AZZ532.\n\nCA...Extreme Heat Watch from Wednesday morning through Thursday \n     evening for CAZ566-567.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Benedict\nLONG TERM...Benedict\nAVIATION...18\nFIRE WEATHER...Benedict\n",
